{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /**
     * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     * The project a language server discovers. Checks everything, emits
     * nothing. `tsconfig.build.json` is the one that emits.
     * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     *
     * The filename is the entire reason this file is shaped the way it is. A
     * TypeScript language server finds a project only through a file *named*
     * `tsconfig.json` — any other name is a config that only a command line
     * will ever pass with `-p`. So a file this config `exclude`s belongs to no
     * project at all: the editor checks it in an **inferred** project with
     * default options — no `paths`, no `types`, and critically no `strict`.
     *
     * This package used to exclude `src/**\/*.test.ts` here and hand them to a
     * `tsconfig.test.json`, with `pnpm typecheck` invoking both by hand. CI was
     * green and correct, and the editor was checking every test file in the
     * package against the wrong compiler options with nobody able to see it.
     * `apps/web` had the identical arrangement and it cost 117 phantom errors
     * there; here it cost the opposite and worse — **silence**. These tests
     * import relatively (`./common.js`) and pull their matchers from `vitest`
     * explicitly, so they need neither `paths` nor ambient types and produced
     * no visible errors at all. An editor reporting nothing while checking
     * without `strict` is the blind-spot shape CLAUDE.md files under "a check
     * that passes over a blind spot is worse than no check".
     *
     * So: this config includes the tests, and the emit settings moved out to
     * `tsconfig.build.json` rather than the tests moving out to a name no
     * editor looks for.
     *
     * ### `types: ["node"]` here, and `types: []` in `tsconfig.build.json`
     *
     * The no-ambient-types guarantee this package depends on — it is imported by
     * the browser app, by the API and workers under node, and by `@flux/mocks`
     * as *source*, so it must typecheck under the intersection of all three
     * environments, which is the bare language — **now lives in
     * `tsconfig.build.json` alone**. `pnpm typecheck` runs both configs, so CI
     * still enforces it; this one does not.
     *
     * That is a real loss and not a tidy-up, so here is the measurement that
     * forced it. The first version of this file declared `types: []` over source
     * *and* tests, on the evidence that the whole package compiled clean that
     * way. That evidence was worthless: excluding the test files from the very
     * same config makes `process.env` in a schema file fail with TS2591 again.
     * The tests were not *surviving* `types: []` — they were **supplying** the
     * node globals, because `import { describe } from 'vitest'` pulls
     * `@vitest/*` declarations that reference `@types/node`, and an ambient type
     * reached by any file in the program is available to every file in it.
     *
     * So a program containing the tests cannot hold the guarantee, and the
     * choice is which of the two the *editor* gets: a discovered project that
     * covers the tests, or one that enforces `types: []` on source. This picks
     * covering the tests, matching `apps/web`, because the failure modes are not
     * symmetrical — an unchecked test file is invisible, whereas a stray
     * `process.env` in a schema file is caught by `pnpm typecheck` and by CI
     * within minutes.
     *
     * `["node"]` is declared rather than left to arrive through vitest. It is
     * already true either way; writing it down means the editor's behaviour does
     * not silently depend on which packages a test happens to import.
     *
     * **The consequence to keep in mind while editing a schema file: this config
     * will not stop you writing `process.env` or `Buffer`.**
     * `tsconfig.build.json` will, and so will CI. If `pnpm typecheck` fails on a
     * line your editor was happy with, that is this trade, working as designed.
     */
    "noEmit": true,
    /** composite + noEmit are incompatible; this config only ever checks. */
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  /**
   * Everything, tests included. Vitest strips types with esbuild and never
   * typechecks, so a test could otherwise reference a field that no longer
   * exists and still pass — the kind of rot that makes people stop trusting
   * the suite.
   */
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["dist", "node_modules"]
}
